.TH SERIAL.H 3v32 "October 15, 2026" "v32dev 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
serial.h \- v32dev serial: a terminal-like stream of words with the host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqserial.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "serial* serial_init( int " channel " );"
.BI "void    serial_free( serial** " sp " );"
.PP
.BI "bool    serial_probe( serial** " sp " );"
.BI "bool    serial_wait_ready( serial** " sp ", int " frames " );"
.BI "bool    serial_ready( serial** " sp " );"
.BI "bool    serial_hup( serial** " sp " );"
.PP
.BI "int     serial_avail( serial** " sp " );"
.BI "int     serial_space( serial** " sp " );"
.BI "int     serial_read( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count " );"
.BI "int     serial_write( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count " );"
.BI "int     serial_write_all( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count " );"
.PP
.BI "int     serial_getc( serial** " sp " );"
.BI "bool    serial_putc( serial** " sp ", int " character " );"
.BI "int     serial_puts( serial** " sp ", int* " text " );"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B serial
is a character device (class
.BR V32DEV_CLASS_CHAR ,
kind
.BR V32DEV_KIND_SERIAL ),
served by the host on a channel of the memory card mailbox (see
.BR v32dev (3v32)).
It is a plain two way stream of words, like a terminal or a serial port. What
is at the host's end is the host's choice: with
.B serial:stdio
the host tool connects it to the terminal that runs it (the lowest 8 bits of
each word are a character), and with
.B serial:echo
it sends back what it receives. The core
.BR v32dev.h (3v32)
moves the words; this header adds the conveniences of a terminal, and includes
it.
.PP
A word is a character, or whatever the program and the host agree on: the
stream does not change the words. There are no messages and no boundaries.
.PP
Nothing blocks, except the functions that say so:
.B serial_wait_ready
and
.BR serial_write_all ,
which end frames while they wait. The rest takes what fits and returns how
many words it moved, so a program can go on drawing.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "serial_init( int " channel )
Create a serial device on a channel (0 to 3). Returns
.B NULL
if the channel is not valid or memory runs out. It is usable once a probe says
so (see
.BR serial_wait_ready ).
.TP
.BI "serial_free( serial** " sp )
Close the channel, release the instance and set the pointer to
.BR NULL .
.TP
.BI "serial_probe( serial** " sp )
Look at the device for this frame. Call it
.B once on every frame.
Returns true when the device became or stopped being ready.
.TP
.BI "serial_wait_ready( serial** " sp ", int " frames )
End frames, probing each time, until the device is usable. Gives up after the
given number of frames and returns false.
.TP
.BI "serial_ready( serial** " sp )
True if the device was usable in the latest probe.
.TP
.BI "serial_hup( serial** " sp )
True if the host hung up (it says there will be no more words) and everything
it sent has been read. Also true if there is no instance.
.TP
.BI "serial_avail( serial** " sp )
Words that can be read now.
.TP
.BI "serial_space( serial** " sp )
Words that can be written now (up to 2023).
.TP
.BI "serial_read( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count )
Take up to
.I count
words. Returns how many, which may be 0.
.TP
.BI "serial_write( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count )
Give up to
.I count
words. Returns how many were taken, which may be 0 if the host is not keeping up.
.TP
.BI "serial_write_all( serial** " sp ", int* " buffer ", int " count )
Give all
.I count
words, ending frames while the host makes room. Gives up, returning how many
words were taken, if the device stops being ready.
.TP
.BI "serial_getc( serial** " sp )
Take one word, or \-1 if there is none.
.TP
.BI "serial_putc( serial** " sp ", int " character )
Give one word. Returns false if the host has no room for it.
.TP
.BI "serial_puts( serial** " sp ", int* " text )
Give a text: an array of words ended by 0, as in the Vircon32 string library
(the 0 is not sent). Waits for room, as
.BR serial_write_all .
Returns how many words were sent.
.PP
Every function that takes a
.I serial**
accepts a pointer to
.B NULL
and then does nothing (returning 0 or false, and true for
.BR serial_hup ).
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
.B struct serial
A serial device. A program should not need to touch its fields; they are
.I dev
(the channel, see
.BR v32dev.h (3v32))
and
.IR ready .
.SH EXAMPLES
Echo to the host what the host types, one word at a time, and say hello first.
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "serial.h"

void main()
{
    serial* port = serial_init( 1 );
    int hello = 0;
    int key = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        serial_probe( &port );
        clear_screen( color_black );

        if( serial_ready( &port ) )
        {
            if( !hello )
            {
                serial_puts( &port, "hello from Vircon32" );
                serial_putc( &port, 10 );
                hello = true;
            }

            key = serial_getc( &port );
            while( key >= 0 )
            {
                serial_putc( &port, key );
                key = serial_getc( &port );
            }
            print_at( 10, 10, "connected" );
        }
        else
        {
            hello = false;
            print_at( 10, 10, "waiting for the host" );
        }

        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
The stream has no end of line or other translation: the host sends and expects
the words as they are, and the host tool uses the lowest 8 bits of each word
as a character. The 10 above is a line feed.
.PP
If the host is killed and comes back, the rings are emptied and the device
becomes ready again by itself; words that were waiting are lost.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32dev.h (3v32),
.BR storage.h (3v32),
.BR v32dev (3v32),
.BR vircon32 (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
